* Seasonal Plot
::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- **Definition**: _Seasonal plot_ allows understanding the model structure over
  time
  - Partition the time-series based on the periodicity:
  - Plot each time series chunk on the same graph

- **Key idea**: Assume you know the periodicity of a signal
  - E.g., yearly periodicity of a monthly time series

::::
:::: {.column width=45%}
![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L10.Antidiabetic_drug_sales_seasonal_plot.png)
::::
:::

- **Question**:
  - Do the data exhibit a seasonal pattern?
  - Is there a within-group pattern (e.g., Jan and July exhibit similar
    patterns)?
  - Are there outliers after accounting for seasonality?
  - Is the seasonality changing over time?

* Seasonal Differencing
::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}
- **Definition**: _Seasonal differencing_ entails taking the difference between
  observations at the same point of consecutive periods
  - E.g., for time series with yearly periodicity, take the Year-over-Year (YoY)
    difference

- **Goal**: Remove seasonal effects in time series data with strong seasonal
  patterns
  - E.g., retail sales or temperature data
  - Help identify underlying trends by eliminating seasonal fluctuations
::::
:::: {.column width=50%}
![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L10.Antidiabetic_drug_sales_subseasonal_plot.png)
\begingroup \small
Sub-seasonal plot
\endgroup
::::
:::

* Spectral Plot
- **Definition**: _Spectral plot_ estimates spectral density of a process from
  time samples of the signal
  - Detect periodicity
  - Identify dominant frequencies
  - Analyze power distribution over frequency

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- **Example**: A spectral plot identifies different frequencies in a sound
  recording
  - Noise reduction or enhancement of certain frequencies
  - Speech recognition
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L10.Speech_spectrogram.png)
::::
